 \section{\uppercase{From Questions to Dashboards}}
 \label{sec:system}
-\noindent Our approach connects flexible analysis selection with checked calculations and reusable views (Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). The model receives the person, offering, cutoff, periods, task family, question, and shared feature/reference definitions. Structured requests let it run analytical batches, inspect compact results, and request more information or finish. Evaluation-only requirements remain hidden, and generated analysis code is not executed.
+\noindent The model receives the person, offering, cutoff, periods, task family, question, and shared feature/reference definitions. Structured requests let it run analytical batches, inspect compact results, and request more information or finish. Evaluation-only requirements remain hidden, and generated analysis code is not executed.
+
+\subsection{Evidence and Answer Validation}
+Evidence records retain features, units, periods, counts, estimates, support, observation and assessment states, uncertainty, and source/configuration hashes. The model receives summaries rather than raw rows. Each peer comparison is identified by $I_{\mathrm{peer}}=(i,o,t,f,W_f,g,W_g)$: person, offering, cutoff, feature, focal period, reference group, and reference period. A personal comparison instead uses $I_{\mathrm{personal}}=(i,o,t,f,W_b,W_r)$, omitting peer meaning. Identifiers are generated only for returned evidence, without hidden expected answers.
+
+Unknown, ambiguous, or altered identifiers are rejected without substitution. Repeated personal summaries are equivalent only when their person, feature, units, cutoff, periods, means, counts, change, and support match exactly. One returned identifier is selected consistently and its alternatives preserved.
+
+Validation independently checks summaries, trajectories, counts, intervals, units, groups, periods, and eligibility against trusted development data. Changing values and recomputing their hash cannot bypass these checks. Numerical consistency does not establish answer completeness.
+
+\subsection{Building and Tracing Answers}
+Selected peer comparisons become claims and panels; personal results become claims and trajectories. Support rules determine whether wording describes a result or explains insufficient observations. Unknown grades remain unavailable measurements. The compiler adds required timelines, observation and assessment context, and qualifications without filling in missing comparison or personal claims. These additions can still answer a requested item, such as grade availability. Provenance records model selections, identifier resolution, metadata, and compiler additions separately. All numerical calculation and insertion are deterministic.
+
+\subsection{Adaptive Presentation of Saved Answers}
+The publication views reorganize saved content after the experiment. They introduce no evidence, corrected omissions, or revised scores. Layout follows content rather than case identifiers. Equivalent personal duplicates may be combined with original multiplicity recorded; distinct peer claims remain available. Evaluation uses complete original dashboards.
+
+Figure~\ref{fig:adaptation} shows personal and limited-observation views. All views retain expandable evidence, zero/unavailable distinctions, and intervals for peer means only. Figure~\ref{fig:reference} switches between saved model-selected comparisons: values, counts, labels, charts, and evidence links update together without recalculation or new analysis.
 
