+\subsection{Post Hoc Statistical and Data-Quality Checks}
+\label{sec:sensitivity}
+To address statistical comparison, we applied two-sided exact McNemar tests to paired complete-visible outcomes, with Holm adjustment across all six condition pairs. The unit is the person, not the 96 method/case slots. C--A and B--A each differ by $-25$ percentage points (adjusted $p=0.2813$); C--B differs by zero ($p=1$). Against enumeration, A differs by $-12.5$ points ($p=0.5000$), and B and C each by $-37.5$ points ($p=0.0234$ each). Under the conditional B17--20 credit, B differs from enumeration by $-20.8$ points ($p=0.3125$ after the same six-test adjustment). These exploratory tests assume independent paired cases; shared course/reference pools and structural selection limit inferential interpretation. Non-rejection does not establish equivalence or held-out reliability.
+
+Using preserved features with exact repeated source rows removed, we repeated the numerical comparisons for the same 24 people, profiles, and 81 peer exclusions. Of 52 required feature/reference records, all 29 supported click-rate peer contrasts and all 13 supported personal changes retained their signs. Median/maximum absolute peer-contrast shifts were 0.167/0.324 clicks per eligible day; the maximum personal-change shift was 1.393. All 16 assessment records, support statuses, and eight cross-reference direction conclusions were unchanged. In Figure~\ref{fig:reference}, +0.043 and $-3.480$ become +0.088 and $-3.335$. This post hoc check does not identify unique click events or establish robustness of agent selections. Intervals were not recomputed; no agents were rerun and completeness scores were unchanged.
+
+Across 24 cases, enumeration took 35.40~s, compared with 484.64, 504.21, and 536.32~s for A, B, and C. These descriptive episode totals include failed runs and repairs but exclude shared model loading; enumeration used no inference.
 
 \section{\uppercase{Discussion and Limitations}}
 \label{sec:discussion}
-\noindent The study demonstrates a practical architecture for combining agent selection, deterministic analysis, and adaptive dashboard views. Its central methodological contribution is making the path from a question to its displayed answer inspectable. This allows developers to distinguish calculation errors, unsuitable selections, missing answers, and context added automatically by the software. Case~15 illustrates the value of this separation. The agent identifies insufficient personal observations but selects earlier peer comparisons for a question about recent activity. Preserving that output exposes a specific selection problem that automatic completion would conceal.
